msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30022"
msgid "The YouTube TV web player version this addon claims to be. Google bumps it regularly; a stale value is the likeliest cause of sudden sign-in failures."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30023"
msgid "Licence proxy port"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30024"
msgid "Local port for the Widevine licence proxy. Change it only if something else on this machine already uses it."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30025"
msgid "Visitor ID (set automatically)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30026"
msgid "Proof-of-origin token"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30027"
msgid "Google requires a proof-of-origin token on media requests. Without one the CDN refuses every segment. Tokens are tied to a browser session and expire after a few hours."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30033"
msgid "Compare YouTube TV client identities"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30034"
msgid "Before playing, asks every YouTube TV client (web, Android, iOS, TV) for the same video and logs what each is offered. Used to find a client served plain URLs rather than SABR."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30037"
msgid "JavaScript runtime"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30038"
msgid "Full path to node, deno, bun or quickjs. Needed to compute the n parameter every media URL carries; without it playback is refused. Leave empty to search PATH and the usual locations. Set it explicitly when Kodi is a flatpak or on LibreELEC, where the runtime lives somewhere Kodi does not look."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30035"
msgid "Resume where you left off"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30036"
msgid "Start a title at the position YouTube has for this account, the same one the web player resumes from. It comes back with the stream, so nothing is stored on this machine. Live channels always start at the live edge."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30039"
msgid "Report progress to YouTube"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30040"
msgid "Send the playing position to YouTube while a title plays, so it counts as watched and resumes here and on every other device. Turn this off to watch without it reaching your account."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30045"
msgid "Integration"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30046"
msgid "Install IPTV Manager add-on"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30047"
msgid "Enable IPTV Manager integration"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30048"
msgid "Publish the YouTube TV channel list and guide to IPTV Manager, so the channels appear in Kodi's own TV section with a full EPG instead of only inside this add-on."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30049"
msgid "IPTV Manager settings…"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30050"
msgid "IPTV Manager channels endpoint"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30051"
msgid "IPTV Manager EPG endpoint"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30052"
msgid "Account"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30053"
msgid "Google API client ID"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30054"
msgid "Signing in needs a Google API project. Three places are tried in order: these two settings, then the YouTube add-on's own project if that add-on is set up (it is the same pair, and this add-on spends none of its quota -- the calls go to tv.youtube.com with a token, never to googleapis.com), then one built into the build. Fill these in only if none of that applies: create a project at console.cloud.google.com, enable the YouTube Data API v3, and make an OAuth client ID of type \"TVs and Limited Input devices\"."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30055"
msgid "Google API client secret"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30062"
msgid "Paste this together with the proof-of-origin token, from the same browser capture. A session-bound token is bound to the visitorData that minted it: the addon bootstraps its own visitorData from the page it loads, which is a different session from your browser's, so a token minted there can never satisfy urls bound to this one. Same account, different session identity, and the media is refused with an empty-bodied 403."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30063"
msgid "SABR endpoint override"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30064"
msgid "Diagnostic. A url pasted here is used instead of the one the player response carries, exactly as pasted. Paste one a browser was served and this answers whether the urls we are issued are refused or our client is: the same url that returned megabytes to the browser either works here or it does not. Leave empty for normal playback."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30065"
msgid "Cold start the proof-of-origin token"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30066"
msgid "Nothing in this addon needs a JavaScript runtime any more: n is solved by the bundled Python interpreter, and the proof-of-origin token is minted by running BotGuard's own interpreter inside a JavaScript engine written in Python. Turn this on to skip the mint and compute a token arithmetically instead -- it costs nothing and works while YouTube reports StreamProtectionStatus 2, but it is good for thirty minutes rather than twelve hours. It also stops n falling back to a runtime if one happens to be installed, so this is the switch for seeing exactly what a box with Python and nothing else does."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30067"
msgid "Least quality to accept on the bridge"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30068"
msgid "The SABR endpoint serves the height you ask it for, and asking is three fields rather than one -- a height, a viewport and a capability list. With those it serves HD, so this is a floor rather than a workaround: set it and the bridge offers nothing shorter, stepping down only if the title has nothing that tall. An earlier version of this note said the endpoint would only serve the tier the player response authorised, which reads AUDIO and SD on every title measured. That was wrong: the browser reads the same and is served HD."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30069"
msgid "Whatever the server picks"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30070"
msgid "720p or better"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30071"
msgid "1080p or better"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30072"
msgid "Audio rendition"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30073"
msgid "Which of YouTube TV's audio tracks to ask the SABR endpoint for. It is applied to the offer, not to the manifest: the endpoint chooses from whatever it is given, so the only way to be served a rendition is to offer nothing else -- and if it refuses an offer of one, playback fails rather than quietly widening. Leave on automatic unless you are testing which rendition a decoder can handle, or you want AC-3 for a receiver. Not every title carries every itag; 381 was absent from the one this was written against."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30074"
msgid "Automatic (best available)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30075"
msgid "150 -- AAC-LC, high"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30076"
msgid "149 -- AAC-LC, medium"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30077"
msgid "148 -- HE-AAC, low"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30078"
msgid "381 -- AC-3"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30079"
msgid "Hide the clear first audio fragment"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30080"
msgid "Diagnostic for inputstream.adaptive 21, and it puts audio out of step with video by about ten seconds -- do not leave it on. YouTube TV's audio arrives with its first fragment unencrypted and everything after it encrypted; on ISA 21.5.22 the audio is destroyed the moment the first encrypted fragment is decoded, and on 22.3.20 the same media plays. Reading both versions found one difference this shape reaches: ISA 21 injects a synthetic senc box into any fragment carrying no saiz, saio or senc, while ISA 22 does so only when the track says its samples are protected by default -- and a clear fragment is exactly such a fragment. Turning this on never hands ISA the clear one. If the audio then decrypts, the fault is that transition and a proper fix is possible; if it is still noise, ISA 21 cannot decrypt this audio and nothing here will change it."
msgstr ""
